description:
Found by Fable`s s503 review probe of s501b (`~/pcl-agent-scratch/s503/review/m5.pl`, r503b-evalpkg2.pl row 6).  PRE-EXISTING: base a0899ba6 and the s501b tree answer the same.

Reproducer (perl prints `D lex-d`, PCL prints `D pkg-d`):

    use strict;
    my $d = "lex-d";
    $Other::d = "pkg-d";
    sub f4c { my $x = $d; eval q{package Other; $d} }
    print "D ", f4c(), "\n";

Remove the line `$Other::d = "pkg-d";` and PCL is right (that is #2285, fixed by s501b: an identity-promoted file lexical publishes its eval capture pair, so the eval`s compiler knows `$d` is a lexical and the `package Other;` in the eval text does not re-home it).  With a QUALIFIED global of the same bare name written anywhere in the file, the pair is not published and the eval reads `$Other::d`.

Suspect (NOT measured): `_promote_captured`s identity branch is guarded by `!$self->{_file_pkg_global}{$canon}`; the cheap discriminating measurement is to dump `_file_pkg_global` for this file -- if `$d` is in it because of the `$Other::d` token (a qualified name in ANOTHER package is not the main-package global `$d`), the fix is to key that table by the QUALIFIED name; if not, the non-identity (renamed `$d__file__N`) route is what fails to publish its pair when the sub also holds a string eval.  Same family: #2557 (`our` alias not seen by a package-switching eval), #2285 (closed by s501b).

Bar: the reproducer + the three spellings in m6.pl (eval in the capturing sub, in another sub, at file level) identical to perl; guard rows in Pl/t/eval-capture-01.t; a scoping change => the full sweep IS the gate; gate-SET scan over both populations.
